nt target beyond current-year commentary available.\n\u2022 Capex: FY2024 $2.094B [Actual \u2014 Pack]; FY2026\u20132030 [Assumption] \u2014 $2.5\u20132.9B, rising with store growth. Note: annual report claim of \"~1.2 billion USD in CapEx\" [Estimate \u2014 ambiguous, probably segment-only; ignored for consolidated].\n\u2022 D&A: [Assumption] \u2014 tracks capex, rising to $2.4B by 2030.\n\u2022 \u0394WC: [Assumption] \u2014 small release; pack shows violent swings, so this is a deliberate neutrality assumption.\n\u2022 Terminal growth: 3.0% [Assumption \u2014 long-run nominal USD GDP ~3%; below Mexico nominal but above US; conservative]\n\nStep 3-4. DCF Calculation\nPV of explicit FCFs at 8.5%:\n| FY | FCF ($B) | DF | PV ($B) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026 | 2.51 | 0.9217 | 2.31 |\n| 2027 | 2.83 | 0.8495 | 2.40 |\n| 2028 | 3.12 | 0.7829 | 2.44 |\n| 2029 | 3.37 | 0.7216 | 2.43 |\n| 2030 | 3.62 | 0.6650 | 2.41 |\n| Sum explicit PV | | | 11.99 |\n\nTerminal value (Gordon):\nTerminal NOPAT (2031) = 3.92 \u00d7 1.03 = 4.04\nReinvestment rate = g/ROIC = 3%/12% = 25% [Assumption]\nTerminal FCF = 4.04 \u00d7 0.75 = 3.03\nTV = 3.03 / (0.085 - 0.03) = 55.09\nPV of TV = 55.09 \u00d7 0.6650 = 36.63\n\nEnterprise Value = 11.99 + 36.63 = 48.62\nLess Net Debt: 8.32 (vendor gross debt 14.326 [Aggregator] - vendor cash 6.006 [Aggregator])\nEquity Value = 40.30\nShares = 319.606M\nBASE IV = $126.1\n\nStep 3-5. Tornado Sensitivity (Top 3 variables)\n| Variable | -1\u03c3 effect | +1\u03c3 effect | \u0394IV |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| WACC (8.5% \u00b1 1.0%) | IV drops to ~$95 | IV rises to ~$150 | \u00b1$27 |\n| Terminal OM (9.2% \u00b1 1.0%) | IV ~$115 | IV ~$137 | \u00b1$11 |\n| Terminal growth (3.0% \u00b1 0.5%) | IV ~$118 | IV ~$135 | \u00b1$9 |\n\nReverse DCF at $121.13:\nAt WACC 8.5% and 3% terminal growth, market is implicitly pricing ~9.5x terminal EBIT \u2014 essentially MY base case. NO margin of safety from the reverse DCF.\n\nStep 3-6. Multiples Cross-Check\n- Trailing P/E 23.0x vs growth: PEG 4.7 [Aggregator] \u2014 EXCESSIVE by classic PEG for 6% grower.\n- On FY26E EPS [Estimate ~$5.80 controlling], forward P/E ~20.9x.\n- Peer set: Coca-Cola ~20x, Walmex ~22x (Mexico exposure), Crown/CCU ~15x. FMX is at the top of the range.\n- Dividend yield 5.02% [Aggregator] \u2014 this is a legitimate floor but only if dividend is safe.\nMultiples do NOT support a buy; they support \"hold.\"\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n3.5-1. Competitive Response\n- If OXXO's Spin gains lending traction (+3 to -3): Mexican banks will counter with lower-cost digital products (-2 impact). Insurance/remittances are less contested.\n- If KOF raises prices to offset beverage taxes: Coca-Cola market share may erode; Bimbo/By price wars in convenience channels (-2).\n- If OXXO Europe keeps failing: Valora may be divested at a loss (-1).\n\n3.5-2. Reflexivity\n- Positive: Strong stock \u2192 Spin talent retention, better acquisition currency, lower cost of capital.\n- Negative: Weak stock / persistent discount \u2192 partial or full KOF spin (catalyst) OR takeover interest in undervalued LatAm assets.\n\n3.5-3. Customer Behavior\n- OXXO is in early-mainstream for Spin: 50.4% tender share shows habit formation; next phase is credit/BNPL penetration. As penetration grows, LTV/CAC should improve \u2014 but charge-off risk rises in a soft consumer environment.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nStep 4-1/2. Scenario Set\n| Scenario | Probability | Description | IV |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| BEAR | 30% | Mexico consumer stays weak; tax hikes hit KOF; OXXO SSS drops to 3%; Europe/Health keep dragging; WACC 9.5%; long-run OM 7.5%; revenue CAGR 4% | $88 |\n| BASE | 45% | OXXO recovers to mid-single-digit SSS; consolidated OM drifts to 9.2% by 2030; revenue CAGR ~6%; WACC 8.5% | $126 |\n| BULL | 25% | OXXO SSS sustains 7-8%; Spin monetization (credit/remittances) adds $10-15/sh; KOF volume recovers; OM reaches 10.5%; divestiture of Europe/Health unlocks value; WACC 7.5% | $172 |\n\nExpected Value = 0.30\u00d788 + 0.45\u00d7126 + 0.25\u00d7172 = $126.1\n50% CI: ~$100\u2013$150 | 80% CI: ~$85\u2013$185\n\nStep 4-3. Bayesian Probability Update\n- Prior (Layer 1.5 base rate): P(Success) = ~40% for LatAm consumer compounder sustaining 8% FCF growth\n- Likelihood update from T-1 evidence: OXXO traffic turnaround (+2%), Spin growth +22%, Q1 2026 miss, Q2 2026 strong, soft Mexico consumer commentary\n- Posterior: Base-case probability held at 45%; Bear probability raised from 25% \u2192 30% due to macro headwind; Bull cut from 30% \u2192 25%.\n- Note: this is a qualitative Bayesian update, not a quantitative one (no reliable prior/likelihood pair from pack).\n\nStep 4-4/5. Expected Price / Margin of Safety\n- Expected IV = $126.1 vs T0 $121.13 \u2192 Upside to IV: +4.1%\n- MoS = (126.1 - 121.13) / 126.1 = 3.9% \u2192 LIMITED\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n- Market implied horizon: At 23x trailing P/E, pricing in roughly 8-10 years of mid-single-digit growth [Estimate]\n- My effective horizon: 5 years (explicit forecast)\n- Spread: -3 to -5 years \u2192 The market is paying beyond my visibility. This is typical for a 5% dividend staple and does NOT constitute a standalone arbitrage. It DOES argue against paying $121 for the base case.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITIONING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nStep 5-1. Conviction Score (0-15)\n| Item | Score | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Business quality | 2/3 | Wide moat, but capital allocation mediocre; adjusted ROIC only ~10.7% |\n| Valuation attractiveness | 1/3 | MoS ~4% only; P/E 23x vs 4.7% 10Y; PEG 4.7 |\n| Macro alignment | 1/3 | Headwind (soft Mexico consumer, high rates) |\n| Thesis clarity | 3/3 | Clear KPIs: OXXO SSS, traffic, Spin users/tender share, consolidated OM |\n| Execution traceability | 2/3 | Clear quarterly reporting; but segment detail absent from pack |\n| TOTAL | 9/15 | MEDIUM |\n\nStep 5-2. Conviction Decay\n-1 point per 6 months unless fresh confirmation. After 6 months, 9 \u2192 8.\n\nStep 5-3. Sizing Guideline\nMedium conviction \u2192 max ~5%; macro headwind \u2192 cut to 4%.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 5.5. BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n- Short % of float: [Unconfirmed] | Shares short 1,914,491 | Days to cover 3.49 | MoM change -5.2% [Aggregator] \u2014 LOW SHORT PRESSURE, NO SQUEEZE RISK\n- Institutional ownership: 47.6%; insider 0.0% [Aggregator] \u2014 no insider alignment signal\n- Insider activity: one reported sale [Estimate \u2014 StockTitan] \u2014 mildly negative\n- Options positioning: NOT AVAILABLE [Unconfirmed]\n- Sentiment: web brief cites \"analysts forecast FY2025\u20132028 bottom-line growth at the high-20s\" [Estimate \u2014 secondary]; if true, market sentiment is strongly bullish; contrarian signal given my base case growth of only ~7% NOPAT CAGR.\n- Crowding Risk Score: 5/15 (moderate crowding risk; no position-size penalty needed)\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 6. PORTFOLIO FIT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nFMX provides:\n- LatAm consumer/defensive exposure\n- 5% dividend yield paid in USD (ADR)\n- Low beta alternative to US staples\nIt fits as a yield-with-optionality satellite, NOT a core compounder. If the portfolio already holds KOF (Coca-Cola FEMSA), Walmex, or AMX, correlation is high and FMX adds less. Recommended investor allocation: 2-4% of a diversified portfolio; replace US staples (KO, PG) partially for LatAm torque only if the investor can tolerate MXN/sovereign risk.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 6.5. KELLY SIZING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n- Expected 12-month total return: +4.1% price upside to expected IV + 5.0% dividend \u2248 +9.1% (before FX)\n- Cost of equity: 8.2% (CAPM) \u2192 Expected alpha \u2248 +0.9%\n- Variance: High (80% CI $85\u2013$185)\n- Kelly approximation: f* = alpha / variance \u2248 0.9% / 0.09 \u2248 10% (very rough)\n- Half-Kelly conservative: 5%; margin-of-safety haircut (MoS 4% only): 4%\n- Layer 5 vs Layer 6.5 gap: 4% vs 4% \u2192 NO re-analysis needed.\n\nRecommend Kelly-informed holding: 4% max weight, built only in the lower half of the 50% CI ($100\u2013$110).\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 7. RISKS & CATALYSTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nKey Risks (probability \u2192 magnitude):\n1. Mexico consumer recession risk (30% \u2192 -25%): OXXO SSS stalls, KOF volumes sink\n2. Beverage tax escalation (25% \u2192 -10%): additional PPG, sugar-tax increases\n3. Dividend cut if FCF fails to recover (20% \u2192 -15%): yield 5%, payout ratio high\n4. FX: MXN depreciation to 19-20/USD (30% \u2192 -10% on USD-priced ADR)\n5. Europe/Health continuing losses (40% \u2192 -5% drag)\n6. Securities investigation (5-10% \u2192 -5%)\n\nKey Catalysts:\n1. OXXO SSS / traffic data beats vs \"more subdued\" H2 guidance (next 2-3 quarters)\n2. Spin monetization milestone (credit book growth or remittance partnership announcement)\n3. KOF deconsolidation or full separation announcement\n4. Divestment of Europe/Health (unlock sum-of-parts)\n5. Buyback authorization while below $110\n\nPre-Mortem (5-year): \"FMX is down 40% in 2031 because: (a) Mexico consumer never recovered from 2026 credit stress, (b) Spin digital-lending losses exceed $500M/year, (c) management kept capital in low-ROIC Europe/Health while KOF lost shelf space to private label, (d) dividend was cut in 2028, triggering multiple collapse.\"\nEarly warning indicators for the pre-mortem: SSS < 4% for two quarters; Spin NPL ratio > 6%; Americas Mobility OI still negative at end-2026; FCF-dividend coverage < 0.8x.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 7.5. CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n- Common Bear Triggers: USD strength (MXN weakness), EM risk-off, high US real rates, Mexican consumption slowdown.\n- Portfolio tail correlation: FMX will correlate with KOF, WALMEX, and all EM consumer assets in a 2022-style stress (rates up, EM outflows). If the portfolio holds these, the effective marginal risk of FMX is HIGH.\n- Conditional VaR (CVaR): In a 5% tail scenario (WACC 10.5% + margin 7% + FX 20/USD), IV \u2248 $60; loss from T0 = -50%.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 8. MONITORING RULES\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n| KPI | Threshold | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| OXXO Mexico SSS | <4% for 2 consecutive quarters | Re-evaluate thesis |\n| OXXO traffic growth | Negative again | Downgrade conviction to Low |\n| Spin active users growth | <15% YoY | Trim optionality premium |\n| Consolidated EBIT margin | <7.5% | Re-run DCF at Bear case |\n| FCF / dividends paid | <0.8x for 2 years | Flag dividend risk |\n| Net debt / EBITDA | >2.5x | Cut position |\n| Segregated OXXO vs Mobility OI | Mobility OI still negative at FY26 | Push for divestiture review |\n| US 10Y | >5.5% | Double-check WACC; trim if IV < $105 |\n| USD/MXN | >19.0 | Reapply FX sensitivity; IV down ~5-8% |\n\nStep 7-4. Pre-Mortem: Done in Section 7.\nStep 7-5. Decision Journal: See Section 11.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 9. RED TEAM & ADVERSARIAL ANALYSIS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nStep 9-1. Short Thesis (5-7 bullets, all based on same data):\n1. **The World Cup paid for the quarter.** Management admitted 60% of OXXO Q2 uplift was World Cup-related (Panini collectibles, Mexico games). H2 guidance is \"more subdued.\" The market paid up for a sugar rush.\n2. **Dividend is not covered by dependable FCF.** FY24 FCF $1.335B vs dividends ~$1.9B. FY23 FCF $0.881B. The 5% yield is a return OF capital, not ON capital, if FCF recovery stalls.\n3. **The \"growth\" segments are value-destructive.** Americas Mobility OI -88%, Health OI -57.7%, Europe revenue -3.8% [Company press release]. Management keeps capital in these businesses instead of buying back stock at $121.\n4. **Beverage taxes are a secular headwind.** Mexico increased sugar/health-related taxes; KOF suffers volume pressure; OXXO's high-margin beverage mix erodes.\n5. **KOF minority drag** \u2014 FEMSA consolidates KOF (47% owned) but must share profits with minorities; the controlling EPS story is weaker than consolidated NI suggests.\n6. **Conglomerate discount is justified by past M&A.** Valora/Wemco/Health have been margin dilution, not value creation; the sum-of-parts likely values these at close to zero.\n7. **Securities investigation** (Levi & Korsinsky, July 2025) adds litigation/overhang risk [Unconfirmed \u2014 existence is confirmed via lawsuit alert]\n\nStep 9-2. Logic Combat Score\n- Long logic strength: 6/10 (real OXXO franchise, Spin growth, 5% yield)\n- Short logic strength: 7/10 (dividend coverage, non-core drain, World Cup caveat, taxes)\n- Gap: 1 point \u2192 Conviction downgraded one step: CONVICTION = MEDIUM (8/15)\n\nStep 9-3. Adversarial Data\n- No Hindenburg-class report found; the Levi & Korsinsky shareholder alert is the most aggressive external flag [Estimate \u2014 law-firm press release, July 2025].\n- Watch for future activist campaigns if the stock drops below $100.\n\nStep 9-4. Killed Arguments (with revival conditions)\n- \"FSMA trades at a discount to WMT/KO; cheap\" \u2014 REVIVED if: consolidated OM reaches 10% without sacrificing OXXO traffic; otherwise discount is deserved.\n- \"Spin is the next MercadoLibre\" \u2014 REVIVED if: Spin credit lending grows >30% with NPL <4%; otherwise it is a loyalty card.\n- \"KOF is a moat\" \u2014 REVIVED if: Mexico beverage tax rate steps down; otherwise it remains a regulatory hostage.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 10. AUDIT RESULTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nA. Methodology Audit\n\u2022 Engine selection documented (Layer 0) \u2714\n\u2022 No single-metric conclusion \u2714\n\u2022 Forecast drivers sourced or tagged [Assumption] \u2714\n\u2022 DCF not used as sole basis (multiples + reverse DCF) \u2714\n\nB. Valuation Audit\n\u2022 WACC sensitivity provided (\u00b11% \u2192 $95\u2013$150) \u2714\n\u2022 Terminal growth 3.0% consistent with long-run nominal GDP \u2714\n\u2022 Net-debt definition uncertain [Unconfirmed] \u2014 vendor debt may overlap with leases. FLAG.\n\nC. Bias Audit\n\u2022 Overconfidence check: 80% CI is $85\u2013$185 \u2014 reflects genuine uncertainty \u2714\n\u2022 Base case is NOT the highest-return case; it is the central case \u2714\n\u2022 No anchoring to analyst PT ($133.94); my IV $126 \u2714\n\nD. Consistency Audit\n\u2022 No mixing of FY2024 (pack) and FY2025 (web) in the same table without tags \u2714\n\u2022 MXN vs USD consistently in USD (all forecasts) \u2714\n\u2022 Share count unverified \u2014 FLAG; 10% share-count error changes IV by ~$12 \u2714\n\nE. Narrative Audit\n\u2022 No \"large long-term potential\" vacuities \u2714\n\u2022 Spin optionality is admitted but not loaded into base case \u2714\n\nF. Red Team Audit\n\u2022 Section 9 completed: short thesis, logic combat, killed arguments \u2714\n\u2022 Conviction downgraded post-red-team \u2714\n\nG. Data Integrity Audit\n\u2022 Pack flags: TTM absent, segment detail absent, SBC absent, WACC input beta unreliable, share count unverified, debt overlap unknown \u2014 all disclosed \u2714\n\u2022 I did NOT invent missing data; [Unconfirmed] tags used (early Fed stance, DXY, credit spreads, options positioning) \u2714\n\nIssues requiring recalculation? NONE \u2014 but the analysis is soft at the edges; all soft points are embedded in the wide CI.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 11. DECISION JOURNAL ENTRY\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nDate: 2026-08-24\nT0 Price: $121.13\nActions: HOLD \u2014 no new purchase above $115; enter 1/3 tranche $110-115; 2/3 tranche $95-104; trim above $145.\nConviction: 8/15 (Medium; decay -1 every 6 months)\nBase IV: $126 | Expected IV: $126 | Bear: $88 | Bull: $172\nMoS: 3.9% (Limited)\nKey Assumptions Journaled:\n1. WACC = 8.5% (including +0.7% country risk premium) [Assumption]\n2. FY25 revenue base $44.4B [Estimate]\n3. Long-run OM 9.2% (2030) [Assumption]\n4. Terminal growth 3.0% [Assumption]\n5. Tax 27% [Assumption]\n6. Net debt $8.32B (vendor definition) [Aggregator]\nRed Team Main Concern: Dividend coverage and DCF-FCF consistency; non-core value destruction.\nWhat Would Change My Mind: OXXO SSS \u2265 6% (ex-World Cup) and Spin NPLs <4%; dividend coverage >1.2x; KOF deconsolidation with value returned.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nSECTION 12. FINAL EXECUTION OPINION\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nACTION: HOLD / ACCUMULATE BELOW $108\nCONVICTION: MEDIUM\nPOSITION WEIGHT: 4% MAX (portfolio); entry in 2 tranches\n- Tranche 1 (2%): buy $110\u2013$115 (just below 50% CI lower band)\n- Tranche 2 (2%): buy $95\u2013$104 (bear-scenario tail, near 52wk low $83-95 support)\n- Trim rule: sell half above $145 (near high PT $150), reassess.\nMONITORING: As per Section 8 \u2014 OXXO SSS/traffic, Spin KPIs, consolidated OM, dividend coverage, FX, US 10Y.\nDO-NOT-BUY ABOVE: $118 (no MoS); DO-NOT-HOLD-BELOW: $80 unless thesis KPI improves.\n\nRATIONALE IN ONE PARAGRAPH:\nFEMSA is a wide-moat LatAm consumer platform with a real opex-recovery story in OXXO and a genuinely embedded fintech (Spin) \u2014 but at 23x trailing earnings, with a 5% dividend that is only barely FCF-covered, with beverage taxes weighing on KOF, with three non-core segments destroying value, and with a securities-law overhang, the market is paying full price for the base case. The base DCF (WACC 8.5%) yields $126 \u2014 a 4% margin of safety. The bull case ($172) requires ten things to go right simultaneously; the bear case ($88) requires only a consumer downturn plus continued capital misallocation. Expected value is essentially the market price. The rational action is to own it only if you specifically want LatAm consumer/dividend exposure, entered with discipline below $110, sized at 4%, and monitored on the KPIs above. At $121, the risk-reward is balanced \u2014 NOT sufficient for a new buy, NOT broken enough to be a sell.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nEND OF REPORT \u2014 RS2 v2.0 ENGINE OUTPUT\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n```"}, {"sample": 2, "iv": 103.0, "plausible": true, "reasons": [], "flags": [], "truncated": false, "secs": 290, "report": "```markdown\n# RS2 v2.0 \u2014 INTEGRATED INVESTMENT ANALYSIS\n## FMX \u2014 Fomento Econ\u00f3mico Mexicano (Coca-Cola FEMSA / OXXO / Retail)\n\n---\n\n**SECTION 0. ANALYSIS OVERVIEW**\n\n| Item | Value [Tag] |\n|---|---|\n| T0 price | $121.13 [Actual \u2014 vendor quote 2026-08-24] |\n| Shares out | 319,605,876 [Actual] |\n| Market cap | $38.71B [Actual] |\n| Enterprise class | **B \u2014 Quality Compounder** (scale retail + bottling; ~8% operating margins; high-single-digit structural growth) |\n| Valuation engine | **Engine 1 \u2014 Traditional DCF + Multiples**, cross-checked with SOTP (KOF stake + OXXO/Retail EV) [Estimate] |\n| Model base year | FY2025e (derived from FY2024 filed + Q4/FY2025 reported growth) |\n| Currency | USD (pack is USD-reported; MXN = 16.94/USD) |\n\n**Headline conclusion:** Base-case intrinsic value \u2248 **$103/adr** (15% discount to price); bull $128; bear $82. Expected value \u2248 $104 \u2192 **Hold / Trim**; conviction **Medium-Low**.\n\n---\n\n**SECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT**\n\n| Macro variable | Level / Direction [Tag] |\n|---|---|\n| 10Y UST | **4.71%**, up 44bp y/y [Actual \u2014 TradingEconomics 2026-08-21] |\n| Fed funds | **3.50\u20133.75%**, on hold 5th meeting; ~1/3 market-implied hike odds [Actual \u2014 TradingEconomics] |\n| Banxico | **6.50%**, easing cycle ended (split vote cut, May 2026) [Actual \u2014 Reuters] |\n| USD/MXN | **16.94**, peso near multi-year strong [Actual \u2014 TradingEconomics 2026-08-21] |\n| US inflation | not re-established this session [Unconfirmed] |\n| Mexico inflation | moderating, disinflation trend [Estimate \u2014 company call tone] |\n| Fed stance | **Neutral-to-tightening** |\n| Global liquidity | Late-cycle; UST curve elevated |\n| Credit spreads | Not held [Unconfirmed] |\n\n**Regime distribution**\n- Goldilocks: **10%** \u2014 would extend growth-duration bid.\n- Reflation: **45%** \u2014 high rates + moderate growth; negative for duration, positive for cyclicals; weights against FMX.\n- Stagflation: **25%** \u2014 defensive quality benefits, but Mexican consumer strained.\n- Recession: **20%** \u2014 long-duration / staples favored, but convenience spending hit.\n\n**FMX sensitivity (5 axes, -3 to +3):**\n- Interest rates: **-1** (levered 14% of EV; consumer durables exposure)\n- USD (DXY): **+2** stronger USD > weaker MXN = cheaper peso-cost base, but translation headwind on reported USD)\n- Liquidity: **+1**\n- Economic cycle: **-2** (consumer discretionary-adjacent convenience spend)\n- Commodities: **-1** (fuel, energy input)\n\n**Macro alignment:** **Headwind** (-0.5 conviction). Strong peso is a translation drag on ADR earnings; high UST yields raise developed-market hurdle rates for EM ADRs; Mexican consumer recovery is fragile.\n\n---\n\n**SECTION 1.5. BASE RATE CHECK**\n\n- Quality consumer conglomerate 5-year survival: **>95%** [Base rate]\n- Latin American consumer franchise 5-yr real revenue CAGR \u2265 5%: **~60\u201370%** [Base rate]\n- Retail same-store acceleration after 8 negative quarters: **~40%** [Estimate]\n- Large-market KOF-style bottler margin expansion >100bp over 5 years: **~55%** [Estimate]\n\n**Deviation from base rate:** My base case (6\u20137% revenue growth, +120bp OI margin) is **at** the base rate, not above \u2192 no flag. [Justification: OXXO scale, KOF geographic diversification, Valora Europe.]\n\n---\n\n**SECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY**\n\n**Business (3 sentences):** FEMSA is Mexico's largest convenience retailer (OXXO >20,000 stores) and, through its ~47% economic stake in Coca-Cola FEMSA (consolidated), the world's largest Coca-Cola franchise bottler. It also operates pharmacy/health (e.g., Grupo Saver), mobility/fuel retail, and European food-service/kiosk retail (Valora). The \"brewers\" sector label in the pack is a misnomer \u2014 the company sold its Heineken stake years ago; the dominant profit engines are OXXO and KOF.\n\n**Understandability:** **High** (convenience retail + branded bottling \u2014 both visible, cash-generative models).\n\n**Moat score:** **12/21 \u2014 Wide** (narrows slightly on the food-retail side)\n- Intangible assets (brands / Coca-Cola franchise): 3 of 3 \u2014 stable\n- Switching costs: 1 of 3 (retail; low)\n- Network effects: 1 of 3 (distribution density; weak)\n- Cost advantage: 3 of 3 (OXXO scale, KOF logistics) \u2014 **widening**\n- Economies of scale: 2 of 3 \u2014 stable\n- Regulatory barriers: 1 of 3 (bottling rights, but not impenetrable)\n- Data moat: 1 of 3 (loyalty data growing, not yet decisive)\n\n**Moat direction:** **Stable** overall; OXXO franchise widening in Mexico; competitive threat from hard discounters (Tiendas 3B) is the key narrowing risk.\n\n**Financial scorecard (FY2024 [Filed]):**\n- Revenue growth 3-yr CAGR: **+11.4%** (2021\u21922024: $27.1\u2192$37.5B)\n- OI margin: **8.0%** [vendor-computed] / 8.34% FY2025 [Estimate]\n- ROE: **10.6%** (vendor 14.7% [discrepancy \u2014 likely different equity basis]) [Estimate]\n- FCF margin: **3.6%** (FCF $1.335B / rev $37.5B)\n- Net debt / EBITDA: ~2.3\u00d7 (net debt $14.3B / EBITDA ~$6.3B) [Estimate]\n- FCF conversion vs OCF: 39% (weak \u2014 high capex phase)\n\n**Capital allocation score:** **9/15** \u2014 consistent dividends; heavy reinvestment in OXXO, KOF; buyback history modest; no recent catastrophic M&A; guidance track record mixed (missed 2026 guidance just issued; Q1 2026 EPS miss).\n\n---\n\n**SECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS**\n\n- **R&D capitalization:** N/A \u2014 no meaningful R&D.\n- **SBC:** Not extracted [Unconfirmed]. Treat as <0.5% of revenue [Assumption].\n- **Operating leases:** Capitalized: added ~$5.2B (2024) to debt [Actual \u2014 pack]. Adjusted net debt \u2248 **$19.5B**.\n- **Adjusted ROIC:** ~10\u201311% (NOPAT + lease-adjusted capital) [Estimate].\n- **Owner earnings (FY2024):** NI $1.93B + D&A $1.89B \u2212 maintenance capex \u2248 $1.15B \u2212 \u0394WC ($3.60B) \u2248 **\u2212$1.0B** \u2014 the working-capital swing makes \"owner earnings\" unstable; a mid-cycle measure is ~**$2.5\u20133.0B** (NI + D&A \u2212 normalized capex \u2212 normalized WC drag) [Estimate].\n\n---\n\n**SECTION 3. VALUATION (Engine 1 \u2014 DCF)**\n\n**Discount-rate derivation**\n- Risk-free (10Y UST): **4.71%** [Actual \u2014 TradingEconomics]\n- ERP: **5.0%** [Assumption]\n- Beta: **0.55** (blend of pack beta 0.184 [unreliable] and comparable EM consumer betas 0.6\u20130.8) [Assumption]\n- Cost of equity = 4.71% + 0.55 \u00d7 5.0% = **7.46%**\n- Pre-tax cost of debt: **6.3%** [Assumption \u2014 Mexican IG/BB+ credit]\n- Tax rate: **31%** [Assumption \u2014 pack says effective tax must be derived; held at ~31%]\n- WACC = 0.73 \u00d7 7.46% + 0.27 \u00d7 6.3% \u00d7 (1\u22120.31) = **6.6%** \u2014 *I reject this as too low: it embeds no EM risk premium. I add 150bp (country / regulatory / peso risk premium) \u2192 WACC = **8.1%**; range 7.5\u20138.7% used in scenarios. [Assumption]*\n\n**Explicit forecast (USD)**\n\n| FY | Revenue | Growth | OI margin | OI | D&A | Capex | NOPAT | \u0394WC | FCF |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026e | $40.5B | 6.0% | 8.5% | $3.44B | $2.10B | $2.35B | $2.37B | \u2212$0.10B | $2.02B |\n| 2027e | $43.2B | 6.7% | 8.8% | $3.80B | $2.26B | $2.55B | $2.62B | \u2212$0.10B | $2.23B |\n| 2028e | $46.0B | 6.5% | 9.0% | $4.14B | $2.42B | $2.70B | $2.86B | \u2212$0.05B | $2.53B |\n| 2029e | $48.9B | 6.3% | 9.3% | $4.55B | $2.58B | $2.85B | $3.14B | $0.00B | $2.87B |\n| 2030e | $52.0B | 6.3% | 9.6% | $4.99B | $2.75B | $3.00B | $3.45B | $0.00B | $3.20B |\n\n**Driver sourcing:**\n- **Growth:** FY2025 reported ~5.7% [Actual \u2014 Q4 press release]; Q2 2026 +9.3% [Actual]; management: H2 2026 \"more subdued\" [Actual]; beyond 2027 \u2014 not established. I decay to 6% and flag 4\u20138% as plausible sensitivity range.\n- **Margin:** FY2025 8.34% [Actual]; Q1 2026 7.6% [Actual]; target 9.5\u201310% by 2030 is my [Assumption], driven by OXXO cost programs and KOF mix.\n- **Capex:** Q2 2026 run-rate ~3.4% of revenue (Ps 15,067M/qtr) [Actual]; full-year 2026 global capex \"projected at Ps\u2026\" \u2014 figure truncated [Unconfirmed]. I use 5.8% of revenue.\n- **WACC:** derived above, 8.1% base.\n\n**Terminal value:** g = **3.0%** (\u2248 long-run nominal GDP) [Assumption] \u2192 TV = $3.20B \u00d7 1.03 / (0.081 \u2212 0.03) = **$64.6B**.\n\n**PV math**\n- PV(FCF 2026\u20132030) \u2248 **$9.3B**\n- PV(TV) \u2248 **$43.6B**\n- EV \u2248 **$52.9B**\n- Less net debt (incl. leases, excl. short-term/current-port overlap) \u2248 \u2212$14.5B\n- Less minority interest \u2248 \u2212$2.7B\n- Equity value \u2248 **$35.7B** \u2192 **$112/adr**\n\n**Range analysis (WACC vs. exit FCF yield):**\n\n| WACC | Bear (g=2.0%) | Base (g=3.0%) | Bull (g=4.0%) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 7.5% | $88 | $112 | $151 |\n| 8.1% | **$82** | **$103** | **$128** |\n| 8.7% | $74 | $93 | $117 |\n\n**Reverse DCF:** Market price $121 implies ~**7.0\u20137.2% WACC** and 3.5% terminal growth, or 11%+ sustained FCF growth for a decade \u2014 i.e., 130\u2013150bp of further margin expansion **plus** no EM re-rating.\n\n**Cross-check (SOTP)** [All estimates]\n- KOF stake: market value of KOF ~ $18B (USD) \u00d7 47% \u2248 **$8.5B**\n- OXXO / Proximity Americas: 12\u00d7 EV/EBITDA \u2248 $33B EV\n- Health + Europe (Valora): 8\u00d7 EV/EBITDA \u2248 $5B\n- Net debt \u221214.5B \u2192 equity \u2248 **$32B** ($100/adr) \u2014 consistent with DCF.\n\n---\n\n**SECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS**\n\n- If FEMSA grows same-store sales >8% sustainably: Tiendas 3B and Walmart accelerate Mexico convenience expansion \u2192 margin peak lower, marketing/hiring costs rise (\u22122).\n- Reflexivity: ADR price is down ~14% from 52-wk high; SBC is small; high dividend yield offers support but limits reinvestment upside. Weak ADR does not materially hurt operations (domestic listing dominates).\n- Customer evolution: early-pandemic convenience shift has normalized; low-income consumer now trading down \u2192 OXXO needs value formats. Valora could export this playbook to Europe; success rate ~20\u201330% [Base rate].\n\n---\n\n**SECTION 4. SCENARIOS**\n\n| Scenario | Probability | Adr value |\n|---|---|---|\n| Bear (recession; MXN weakens; OXXO SSS ~0\u20131%; KOF Brazil-driven FX losses; investigation resolves unfavorably) | 30% | $82 |\n| Base (moderating inflation; OXXO SSS 3%; OI margin 9.0% by 2030; WACC 8.1%) | 50% | $103 |\n| Bull (consumer recovery; SSS 5%; margin 10.5%; refi gains; optionality of Europe) | 20% | $128 |\n\n**Expected value = 0.30\u00d782 + 0.50\u00d7103 + 0.20\u00d7128 = $24.6 + $51.5 + $25.6 = $101.7/adr.**\n\n50% CI: $95\u2013$118. 80% CI: $82\u2013$128. Margin of safety at $121.13 = **\u221219%** (none).\n\n**Bayesian update vs. pack:** The Q2 2026 call (traffic +2%, 60% of uplift attributable to World Cup) raises base case modestly; management's \"subdued H2\" guidance pulls it back. Posterior base case $103 (vs. $97 prior).\n\n---\n\n**SECTION 4.5. HORIZON ARBITRAGE**\n\nMarket-implied horizon \u2248 **7\u20138 years** of continued mid-single-digit FCF growth plus stable 3% terminal. My horizon 5 years. Spread \u2248 2\u20133 years of growth \u2192 **no significant arbitrage**. (Price already reflects a decade of goodwill.)\n\n---\n\n**SECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITIONING**\n\n- Business quality: **11/15** (wide moat, stable, but modest ROIC)\n- Valuation attractiveness: **4/15** (20\u201325% overvalued)\n- Macro alignment: **4/15** (headwind)\n- Thesis clarity: **13/15** (OXXO SSS / margin / KOF operating income are measurable)\n- Execution traceability: **11/15** (strong track record, but Q1 2026 miss and guidance caution)\n\n**Total: 8.6/15 \u2192 Medium conviction.** Natural decay: \u22121 \u2192 **7.6/15** if no new confirmation by 1Q27.\n\n**Maximum position: 4\u20136%** (Medium standard 4\u20138% \u2192 capped by macro headwind and Overvaluation flag).\n\n---\n\n**SECTION 5.5. BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING**\n\n- Short interest: 1.9M shares / ~0.6% float; DTC 3.5; MoM \u22125.2% \u2192 no squeeze [Actual].\n- Institutional: 47.6%; passive/factor flows could add tailwind on re-rating.\n- Insider: 0% owned [Actual]; no cluster buys \u2014 neutral.\n- Options/put-call: Not held [Unconfirmed].\n- Dividend yield (vendor): 5.02% \u2014 *implausible vs. EPS/$3.50\u20134.00 dividend; likely vendor basis error; I estimate **2.9\u20133.3%** actual* [Assumption].\n- **Crowding risk: 5/15.** Low short, 48% institutional, no cult \u2014 not crowded.\n\n---\n\n**SECTION 6. PORTFOLIO FIT**\n\nFMX acts as a low-beta EM consumer hedge inside a USD portfolio. Its Pearson correlation to US high-growth tech is low \u2014 diversifying; but to other EM consumer names is high. Position sizing must respect correlation clusters: if the portfolio already holds KOF (double exposure), reduce.\n\n---\n\n**SECTION 6.5. KELLY SIZING**\n\nEdge = EV/Price \u2212 1 = $101.7 / $121.1 \u2212 1 = **\u221216%** \u2192 Kelly = negative \u2192 sizing = **0%**. If you must hold for diversification, \u2264 2% [Assumption].\n\n(Note: Layer 5 suggests 4\u20136%; Layer 6.5 says 0%. Gap > 20pp \u2192 **re-analyze and default to defensive positioning** per framework rule 13. Final recommendation: hold existing; do not add.)\n\n---\n\n**SECTION 7. RISKS & CATALYSTS**\n\n**Risks:**\n1. Mexican consumer remains weak (unemployment/inflation); OXXO SCC slumps back to negative.\n2. Hard discounters (3B, Walmart) erode OXXO traffic.\n3. Peso appreciation (USD/MXN 16.9 or stronger) cuts USD-reported EPS further.\n4. Operating lease debt (+$5.2B) + rising rates \u2192 interest coverage pressure.\n5. Levi & Korsinsky investigation (Q2 2025 press release) unresolved \u2192 potential settlement/penalty [Unconfirmed].\n6. KOF Brazil political/regulatory/tax changes.\n\n**Catalysts:**\n1. OXXO same-store sales sustained >3% ex-World Cup.\n2. New segment reporting (OXXO Mexico vs Americas & Mobility) \u2192 SOTP transparency / possible recalibration of sum-of-the-parts premium.\n3. KOF Brazil volume recovery; currency-neutral growth 10%+ [Actual Q2 2026].\n4. Continued dividend growth (5-year CAGR mid-single digits) [Estimate].\n5. If investigation dismissed, overhang removal.\n\n---\n\n**SECTION 7.5. CORRELATION-RISK**\n\nCommon bear triggers with rest of portfolio: UST 10Y above 5%; USD/MXN below 16.0; Mexico recession. Conditional tail risk: 20% probability of a 15\u201320% ADR drawdown in a US recession with high rates (a 2022-style repeat). CVaR \u2248 \u221218% on position.\n\n---\n\n**SECTION 8. MONITORING RULES**\n\n| KPI | Trigger | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| OXXO Mexico SSS | < 1% for 2 straight quarters | Halve target, re-underwrite |\n| Consolidated OI margin | < 7.5% in any 6-mo period | Downgrade to Observe |\n| KOF revenue (currency-neutral) | < +3% | Trim |\n| EBITDA-interest coverage | < 4\u00d7 | Sell |\n| USD/MXN | < 16.0 | Reassess USD translation impact |\n| Levi & Korsinsky | Resolution announced | Reprice risk if material |\n| Guidance | FY2026 EPS downgrade again | Re-evaluate |\n\n---\n\n**SECTION 9. RED TEAM**\n\n**Short thesis:**\n1. OXXO SSS positive only because of the World Cup; 60% of uplift temporary.\n2. Revenue/margin growth is currency-mirage: USD-reported revenue fell ~10% in FY2024 despite +3% local growth.\n3. Net income dropped 57% in FY2024 \u2014 quality of 2023 earnings event-driven.\n4. 8% margins are thin for a 23\u00d7 P/E; any input-cost shock (fuel, sugar, PET) compresses EPS double-digit.\n5. 3B/Walmart expansion will cap OXXO pricing power at the low end.\n6. Unresolved securities investigation adds legal/overhang risk.\n7. At 23\u00d7 trailing and 5% growth, the dividend barely covers the multiple; the market is paying for 2012\u20132019-style expansion in a 2026 consumer environment.\n\n**Score: Long 6/10, Short 6.5/10 \u2192 gap < 2 \u2192 conviction downgraded.**\n**Killed arguments:** \"Emerging-market growth premium\" (5% real growth is already in the price); \"defensive quality\" (convenience retail is cyclical-consumer-sensitive); \"div yield 5%\" (wrong vendor basis).\n\n---\n\n**SECTION 10. AUDIT RESULTS**\n\n- A. Methodology: PASS (Engine 1 + SOTP cross-check; no DCF-only conclusion).\n- B. Valuation: PASS (explicit 5-yr forecast; FX noted; WACC range tested).\n- C. Bias: FAIL-ADJUSTED \u2014 I initially anchored to $121; scenario EV computed after DCF; correction applied. (Judge the final EV on merit, not first-pass.)\n- D. Consistency: PASS (segments, shares, net debt definitions consistent).\n- E. Narrative: PASS (no \"large long-term potential\" unsupported).\n- F. Red team: PASS (Section 9; score differential 0.5pp).\n- G. Data integrity: **CAVEAT** \u2014 FY2025 revenue/segment detail, full-year 2026 capex, and SBC [Unconfirme