sumption]:**\n- Goldilocks: 30% \u2014 would favor the long-duration/quality components of FEMSA\n- Reflation: 35% \u2014 the modal case; rates high, growth moderate; mixed for FEMSA\n- Stagflation: 20% \u2014 would pressure Mexican consumer purchasing power, OXXO ticket growth\n- Recession: 15% \u2014 would hurt volumes but FEMSA is defensive\n- Total = 100%\n\n**Step 1-3. Macro sensitivity (each \u22123 to +3):**\n- Interest-rate sensitivity: \u22121 (24% debt; lease-heavy; consumer staples demand is rate-insensitive)\n- Dollar sensitivity: \u22122 (strong MXN is a headwind for USD-denominated ADR returns; ~60%+ of costs/revenue in MXN) [Estimate]\n- Liquidity sensitivity: 0 (FCF-positive; no dependence on capital markets)\n- Economic-cycle sensitivity: +1 (OXXO is defensive; KOF is stable; Valora/Pharma mildly cyclical)\n- Commodity sensitivity: \u22121 (PET resin, sugar, fuel pass-throughs)\n\n**Step 1-4. Macro alignment:** Weighted macro impact \u2248 \u22120.3 \u2192 **Neutral-to-Headwind** (strong peso + elevated USD rates + inflation stickiness). Conviction \u22120.5 steps. Macro does not change intrinsic value; it delays the reward.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN (v2.0)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 1.5-1. Base rates applicable to FEMSA:**\n- 5-year survival probability for dominant #1 convenience retailer in a top-15 global economy: **>95%** [Assumption, base rate]\n- Retail traffic-turnaround success (8 negative quarters \u2192 sustained positive traffic): **40\u201360%** within 3 years [Assumption, base rate]\n- Margin recovery programs at dominant retail chains delivering +150bps consolidated operating margin in 5 years: **~50%** [Assumption]\n- Large M&A ($1B+, Valora) achieving 5-year ROIC > WACC: **~25\u201335%** [Estimate, framework base rate]\n- Platform/digital monetization success (OXXO to fintech): **~20\u201330%** [Estimate]\n\n**Step 1.5-2. Deviation of this analysis from base rates:**\nMy base case assumes the OXXO reset succeeds at a ~60% pace (traffic turns and consolidates, margin rises from 8.34% FY25 to 9.2% by 2035). That is within 1.0\u20131.2x of the base rate, justified by:\n1. OXXO holds >50% convenience-store share in Mexico; no direct national competitor has matched its density (~22,000 stores) [Estimate]\n2. First positive traffic print in 8 quarters occurred in Q2 2026 (+2.0%), before the World Cup is stripped out \u2014 implying the strategic reset is taking hold [Estimate from earnings excerpt]\n3. KOF is the world's largest Coca-Cola bottler, providing a stable cash-flow floor [Estimate, web brief]\n\n**Step 1.5-3. Base-rate shock check:** My bull case success margin (10.0% by 2030) is ~1.2x the base case \u2014 no red flag. I am NOT pricing in pharma scale-up, fintech optionality, or a Valora turnaround success; if those materialize, upside exists beyond my bull.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY ASSESSMENT\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 2-1. Business understanding (3 sentences):**\n1. FEMSA operates the largest convenience-store chain in Latin America (OXXO, ~22,000 stores [Estimate]) with dominant positioning in Mexico proximity retail.\n2. Through Coca-Cola FEMSA (KOF), it is the world's largest Coca-Cola franchise bottler, and since 2022 it runs Valora, a European convenience/food-service network. [Estimate, web]\n3. It is building a pharmacy vertical (FEMSA Salud), logistics, and a fintech layer (Spin) on top of the OXXO customer base.\n**Understandability: High \u2014 but the consolidated entity is a deliberately mixed conglomerate, which complicates the margin picture.**\n\n**Step 2-2. Moat score: 12/15 (Wide moat) [Assumption \u2014 no segment data in pack]:**\n- Intangible assets (OXXO brand, Coke licenses): 3/3 \u2014 Widening (brand equity) but N/A for Coke's licensed territories\n- Switching costs: 2/3 \u2014 Stable (convenience is habit/routine-driven; high frequency, low consideration)\n- Network effects: 2/3 \u2014 Widening (store density \u2192 distribution efficiency \u2192 assortment advantage)\n- Cost advantage: 2/3 \u2014 Widening (scale procurement, own distribution)\n- Economies of scale: 2/3 \u2014 Stable (KOF is the most efficient bottler in the system)\n- Regulatory barriers: 1/3 \u2014 Stable (liquor licenses, Coke franchise boundaries; low regulatory moat otherwise)\n- Data moat: 0/3 \u2014 Narrowing (OXXO loyalty data exists but not yet monetized; competitors are also building data)\n**Moat direction: OXXO moat Widening (traffic turn, consumer-centric reset); Coca-Cola FEMSA moat Stable; Valora moat weak (3/15) \u2014 net direction: Stable-to-Widening.**\n\n**Step 2-3. Lynch classification: Turnaround-within-a-Stalwart.**\n- Stalwart checkpoints: revenue consistency (5-yr USD CAGR 6.9% [pack]; MXN growth is more stable), dividend growth (+3.7% proposed [Estimate, market-pulse.co]), FCF positive.\n- Turnaround checkpoint: traffic recovered to +2% in Q2 2026 after eight negative quarters [Estimate, earnings excerpt] \u2014 first confirmation data point; needs 4 more quarters of confirmation.\n\n**Step 2-4. Financial strength scorecard [pack filing-derived unless noted]:**\n- Revenue 5-yr USD CAGR: +6.9% [Actual]; FY25 MXN revenue: MXN 840.95B [Estimate, web brief]\n- FY24 gross margin: 41.1% [Arithmetic]; FY25 operating margin: 8.34% [Estimate, web brief]\n- ROE (vendor): 14.7% [Actual]; ROE is depressed by ~MXN 100B+ of low-yield cash/no investments [Assumption]\n- FCF (FY24): $1.335B [Arithmetic]; FCF conversion 39% of NI \u2014 heavy growth-capex year [Estimate]\n- Net debt (my tally): $5.58B [Arithmetic]; net debt/EBITDA \u2248 1.1\u20131.4x [Estimate]; interest coverage \u2248 3.1x on FY24 op-income proxy [Estimate]\n- Net cash: none; but the balance sheet is robust with $6.7B cash [Actual]\n**Verdict: Good (not excellent \u2014 because returns on the full asset base are mediocre at ~6\u20137% GAAP ROIC [Estimate]).**\n\n**Step 2-5. Capital allocation scorecard: 7/15 [Assumption]:**\n- Buyback efficiency: 1/3 \u2014 little evidence of consistent opportunistic buybacks; dividends are the principal return\n- M&A track record: 2/3 \u2014 Heineken divestment (2023) crystallized value; but Valora ($1.15B, 2022) [Estimate, globalbankingandfinance.com] is a negative-ROIC drag\n- Capex efficiency: 2/3 \u2014 new OXXO stores historically 15\u201320%+ cash ROIC [Estimate]; consolidated incremental ROIC is diluted by Valora\n- Rationality of mix: 1/3 \u2014 conglomerate structure complicates capital allocation; no sum-of-the-parts premium\n- Guidance accuracy: 1/3 \u2014 Q1 2026 miss [Estimate, web brief]; qualitative H2 2026 caution\n- Insider ownership bonus: +0 (control-group structure is real but 13F-reported insider = 0.0% [Unconfirmed])\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS (v2.0)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n- R&D capitalization: not meaningful for retail/bottling; omitted.\n- SBC: **not available** in the pack. FEMSA historically runs SBC at 0.3\u20130.5% of revenue [Estimate]; I apply \u22120.4% of revenue as an FCF haircut implicitly via the margin conservatism. [Assumption]\n- Operating leases: capitalized as debt in the DCF (liability MXN 87.8B at 16.94 = $5.18B) [Estimate \u2014 tag interpretation uncertain]\n- Adjusted ROIC: GAAP ROIC \u2248 6\u20137% [Estimate]; adjusted ROIC (adding back brand/density value) \u2248 8\u20139% [Assumption]; marginal ROIC on new OXXO stores is 15\u201320%+ [Estimate]\n- Owner Earnings (FY25, MXN B): NI ~41.4 + D&A ~42.0 \u2212 maintenance capex ~46.3 \u2212 WC release ~2.0 = **MXN 39.1B (~$2.16B)** [Estimate]\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 3. VALUATION ENGINE \u2014 ENGINE 1 (DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Discount-rate derivation (WACC, MXN):**\n- Risk-free (MXN 10Y Mbono): 9.0% [Assumption \u2014 Banxico at 6.50% [Actual] + term premium; direct Mbono quote not retrieved]\n- ERP: 5.5% [Assumption \u2014 Damodaran-style Mexico]\n- Beta: 0.60 [Assumption \u2014 the pack's 0.184 is an unverifiable ADR beta vs an unknown benchmark, and is rejected; a 0.60 levered beta reflects consumer-defensive quality + Mexico country risk]\n- Cost of equity = 9.0 + 0.60 \u00d7 5.5 = **12.3%**\n- After-tax cost of debt = 8.5% \u00d7 (1 \u2212 0.31) = **5.9%** [Assumption]\n- Weights: E = $38.714B; D = $12.288B (incl. leases) \u2192 E/D+E = 75.9%\n- **WACC = 0.759 \u00d7 12.3% + 0.241 \u00d7 5.9% = 10.75% \u2192 10.7%** [Assumption/derived]\n\n**Terminal assumptions:** g = 4.5% MXN nominal [Assumption: \u22482.0% real + 2.5\u20133.0% inflation]; terminal ROIC = 12% [Assumption]; reinvestment rate = g/ROIC = 37.5%.\n\n**Explicit forecast (MXN B) \u2014 sources cited per driver:**\n\n| Year | Revenue | EBIT margin | EBIT | NOPAT (31% tax) | D&A @5.0% | Capex | \u0394WC | FCF |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2025 | 841.0 [Est] | 8.34% [Est] | 70.1 | 48.4 | 42.0 | 46.3 | +2.0 | 46.1 |\n| 2026 | 900.0 [Est] | 8.10% [Est] | 72.9 | 50.3 | 45.0 | 49.5 [Est] | +0.3 | 46.1 |\n| 2027 | 954.0 [Asm] | 8.40% [Asm] | 80.1 | 55.3 | 47.7 | 50.6 | +0.3 | 52.7 |\n| 2028 | 1011.2 [Asm] | 8.70% [Asm] | 88.0 | 60.7 | 50.6 | 51.6 | +0.3 | 60.0 |\n| 2029 | 1061.8 [Asm] | 8.90% [Asm] | 94.5 | 65.2 | 53.1 | 53.1 | +0.3 | 65.5 |\n| 2030 | 1104.3 [Asm] | 9.00% [Asm] | 99.4 | 68.6 | 55.2 | 53.0 | +0.2 | 71.0 |\n| 2031 | 1148.5 [Asm] | 9.00% [Asm] | 103.4 | 71.3 | 57.4 | 52.8 | +0.2 | 76.1 |\n| 2032 | 1182.9 [Asm] | 9.10% [Asm] | 107.6 | 74.3 | 59.1 | 52.0 | +0.2 | 81.6 |\n| 2033 | 1218.4 [Asm] | 9.10% [Asm] | 110.9 | 76.5 | 60.9 | 52.4 | +0.2 | 85.2 |\n| 2034 | 1254.9 [Asm] | 9.20% [Asm] | 115.5 | 79.7 | 62.7 | 52.7 | +0.2 | 89.9 |\n| 2035 | 1292.6 [Asm] | 9.20% [Asm] | 118.9 | 82.0 | 64.6 | 53.0 | +0.2 | 93.8 |\n\n**Source tags per driver:**\n- FY25 revenue MXN 840.95B: **[Estimate]** \u2014 web research brief; cross-consistency: FY26 capex guidance 5.0\u20136.0% of sales [Estimate, finsee.ai] and H1-26 capex MXN 15.0B at 3.4% of sales [Estimate] implies FY26 sales \u2248 MXN 900B, i.e. +7% vs 841B.\n- FY26 revenue growth +7.0%: **[Estimate]** \u2014 consensus revenue growth +7.69% [pack, yfinance]; Q2-26 +9.3% YoY [Estimate, web]; H2 \"more subdued\" [Estimate, management].\n- FY27\u201331 revenue +3\u20136%: **[Assumption]** \u2014 no guidance beyond FY26; I assume OXXO SSS 4\u20136% + net store growth 3\u20135%, partially offset by KOF volumes at low-single-digit and Valora at 2\u20134%. Beyond FY31 the path is not established; the valuation effect of the band is shown in the scenario table.\n- FY25 margin 8.34%: **[Estimate]** \u2014 web research brief.\n- FY26 margin 8.1%: **[Estimate]** \u2014 Q2-26 op-income growth +7.2% vs revenue +9.3% [Estimate] implies compression; H2 subdued.\n- Margin path to 9.2% by 2035: **[Assumption]** \u2014 reflects a *partial* OXXO margin recovery and KOF operating leverage, while Valora and pharmacy remain margin-dilutive. The market's implied margin at $121.13 is ~9.5% by 2035 (reverse-DCF back-calc) \u2014 my base is 30bps lower.\n- Capex: FY26 = 5.5% of revenue **[Estimate]** (company guidance 5.0\u20136.0%, finsee.ai); fade to 4.1% by 2035 **[Assumption]** as store-growth matures.\n- D&A @ 5.0% of revenue: **[Estimate]** \u2014 FY23\u201324 D&A/revenue = 4.84\u20135.03% [Arithmetic on pack].\n- \u0394WC = \u22120.5% of incremental revenue: **[Assumption]** \u2014 history is noisy (pack \u0394WC swings from \u22123.6 to +7.7), interpreted as tag/consolidation-driven; retailers typically run negative WC via supplier float.\n\n**DCF output (MXN B):**\n- PV of explicit FCF (2026\u20132035) at WACC 10.7%: **MXN 425.2B**\n- Terminal value (2035): FCF_t = 53.6; TV = 53.6 / (0.107 \u2212 0.045) = **MXN 864.5B**; PV of TV = **MXN 329.0B**\n- **Enterprise value = MXN 754.2B**\n- Cash: MXN 113.6B [Arithmetic, filed cash 6.705 \u00d7 16.94]; Debt/leases: MXN 208.2B [Arithmetic, filed components]\n- **Equity value = MXN 659.6B** \u2192 per ADR (319.6M): MXN 2,064 \u2192 **$121.80/ADR at USD/MXN 16.94**\n\n**Multiples cross-check:**\n- Implied forward P/E on 2026 NI \u2248 MXN 43B: ~15x; on 2025 NI \u2248 41B: ~16x \u2014 **below** the vendor's 23x trailing, which uses a depressed TTM EPS ~$5.27 [Unconfirmed; possibly the vendor's TTM includes FY24's low NI].\n- EV/EBITDA \u2248 $44.3B / $4.9B \u2248 9.0x [Estimate] \u2014 in line with FEMSA's historical range.\n- Dividend yield: 5.0% [pack, includes extraordinary distributions] \u2014 provides support.\n\n**SOTP cross-check (coarse):**\n- KOF market cap \u2248 $16\u201317B [Estimate, unverified]; FMX stake \u2248 47.6% [Estimate, unverified] \u2192 **$7.6\u20138.0B**\n- Remaining FMX EV \u2248 $44.3B \u2212 $8.0B = **$36.3B** for ~22,000 OXXO stores [Estimate] + Valora + pharma + digital + logistics \u2192 ~$1.65M per OXXO store. A high-quality LatAm convenience store network at replacement cost + franchise value: fair to slightly rich. Cross-check does not contradict the DCF.\n\n**Tornado / sensitivity (top 3 value drivers):**\n1. **2035 operating margin** (range 8.0%\u201310.0%): \u00b1$27/ADR\n2. **WACC** (range 10.2%\u201311.5%): \u00b1$20/ADR\n3. **Terminal g/ROIC** (g 4.0\u20135.0%, ROIC 10\u201314%): \u00b1$12/ADR\n\u2192 Thesis KPIs = OXXO Mexico SSS/traffic, consolidated op margin, USD/MXN.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS (v2.0)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n1. **Competitive response (score \u2212 2):** Tiendas 3B (BBB Foods) is growing store count at >20%/yr [Estimate, market sources] and attacks OXXO's value tier; Walmex convenience formats also scale. If OXXO's reset is a price/quality response, 3B's expansion could keep margin recovery below my base.\n2. **Reflexivity (score +1):** A rising share price supports FEMSA's M&A currency (used for Valora, pharma builds); a falling price (as in 2024\u2013H1 2025, down to $83) pressured sentiment but had minimal operational reflexivity (no debt-driven spiral).\n3. **Customer behavior evolution:** OXXO is positioned at the early-mainstream stage of convenience adoption in Mexico; digital/financial services attach rate is still low. The risk is that lower-income consumers trade down further if the Mexican consumer weakens; the opportunity is ticket growth from foodservice and fresh.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 4. SCENARIO ENGINE (BAYESIAN)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Prior base rates (Section 1.5):** retail turnaround success 40\u201360%; my base assigns ~50\u201355% success.\n\n**Bayesian update triggers (posterior shifts):**\n- OXXO Mexico SSS \u22654% and traffic \u22650% in H2-26 (ex-World Cup): shifts 15pp from Bear to Bull\n- Consolidated op margin \u22658.5% in FY27: confirms margin recovery; shifts 10pp to Bull\n- KOF Mexico volume decline >3% due to excise taxes: shifts 10pp to Bear\n- USD/MXN > 19.0: ADR pressure; MXN per-share value increases (two-way)\n\n**Scenario table:**\n\n| Scenario | Prob | Assumptions | IV/ADR |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bear | 25% | WACC 11.5%; margin stalls at 8.0%; revenue +4%; g 4%; Valora remains a drag; 3B takes share | ~$94 |\n| Base | 50% | WACC 10.7%; margin 8.1%\u21929.2%; revenue +5% avg; g 4.5% | ~$122 |\n| Bull | 25% | WACC 10.2%; margin 8.2%\u219210.0% by 2031; revenue +5\u20136%; g 5%; OXXO reset succeeds | ~$157 |\n\n**Expected price = 0.25\u00d794 + 0.50\u00d7122 + 0.25\u00d7157 = $123.75** [Estimate]\n**Margin of Safety = (123.75 \u2212 121.13)/123.75 = +2.1% \u2192 Limited/None.**\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE (v2.0)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n- Market-implied horizon: At $121.13, the reverse-DCF requires a 2035 operating margin of ~9.5% and growth of ~4.5% \u2014 equivalent to a 8\u201310-year fully-loaded expectation [Estimate].\n- My horizon: 3\u20135 years, with the OXXO reset as the central KPI.\n- Spread: Market horizon (8\u201310y) \u2212 my horizon (5y) \u2248 3\u20135 years. This normally signals an arbitrage; here it is an arbitrage *against* the bulls: the price already capitalizes a successful reset. The payoff to correctness is compressed; there is no free lunch at T0.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u255