in at $121.13 is ~9.5% by 2035 (reverse-DCF back-calc) \u2014 my base is 30bps lower.\n- Capex: FY26 = 5.5% of revenue **[Estimate]** (company guidance 5.0\u20136.0%, finsee.ai); fade to 4.1% by 2035 **[Assumption]** as store-growth matures.\n- D&A @ 5.0% of revenue: **[Estimate]** \u2014 FY23\u201324 D&A/revenue = 4.84\u20135.03% [Arithmetic on pack].\n- \u0394WC = \u22120.5% of incremental revenue: **[Assumption]** \u2014 history is noisy (pack \u0394WC swings from \u22123.6 to +7.7), interpreted as tag/consolidation-driven; retailers typically run negative WC via supplier float.\n\n**DCF output (MXN B):**\n- PV of explicit FCF (2026\u20132035) at WACC 10.7%: **MXN 425.2B**\n- Terminal value (2035): FCF_t = 53.6; TV = 53.6 / (0.107 \u2212 0.045) = **MXN 864.5B**; PV of TV = **MXN 329.0B**\n- **Enterprise value = MXN 754.2B**\n- Cash: MXN 113.6B [Arithmetic, filed cash 6.705 \u00d7 16.94]; Debt/leases: MXN 208.2B [Arithmetic, filed components]\n- **Equity value = MXN 659.6B** \u2192 per ADR (319.6M): MXN 2,064 \u2192 **$121.80/ADR at USD/MXN 16.94**\n\n**Multiples cross-check:**\n- Implied forward P/E on 2026 NI \u2248 MXN 43B: ~15x; on 2025 NI \u2248 41B: ~16x \u2014 **below** the vendor's 23x trailing, which uses a depressed TTM EPS ~$5.27 [Unconfirmed; possibly the vendor's TTM includes FY24's low NI].\n- EV/EBITDA \u2248 $44.3B / $4.9B \u2248 9.0x [Estimate] \u2014 in line with FEMSA's historical range.\n- Dividend yield: 5.0% [pack, includes extraordinary distributions] \u2014 provides support.\n\n**SOTP cross-check (coarse):**\n- KOF market cap \u2248 $16\u201317B [Estimate, unverified]; FMX stake \u2248 47.6% [Estimate, unverified] \u2192 **$7.6\u20138.0B**\n- Remaining FMX EV \u2248 $44.3B \u2212 $8.0B = **$36.3B** for ~22,000 OXXO stores [Estimate] + Valora + pharma + digital + logistics \u2192 ~$1.65M per OXXO store. A high-quality LatAm convenience store network at replacement cost + franchise value: fair to slightly rich. Cross-check does not contradict